@*
    A rating, drawn.

    The marks are gold at every band, which was not true of the glyph version and needs
    saying. Glyphs drew only the stars a match had earned, so two and five differed by width
    alone and a uniform gold genuinely did read as a triumph whatever happened — the band
    colour was the fix for that. Drawn marks always show all five, so an unearned star is a
    hollow one and the comparison lives in the drawing. The band is still here: it colours the
    verdict word, and it keeps the glow for the one rating that has earned it.

    The band comes from `Grades`, which reads the stars, and the score elsewhere on the screen
    converts to stars rather than carrying its own thresholds.

    The marks are drawn rather than typed. `★★★¾` set a vulgar fraction against three glyphs
    at the biggest moment the app has, which reads as a typo rather than as three and three
    quarters — and the fraction's size and baseline were whatever the system font felt like.
    A partial star is now a partial star: one path, clipped at the fraction.

    Rounding stays with `MatchEngineResult.GlyphsFor`, which the console front end and the
    show notes still use. Two views of one rating that round differently is how a result ends
    up reported as ★★★¾ in the log and four stars on screen.
*@
<span class="stars" title="@Grades.OfStars(Rating).Label()">
    <svg class="stars__marks @Grades.OfStars(Rating).Tone()" viewBox="0 0 @Width 20"
         role="img" aria-label="@Rating.ToString("F2") out of 5">
        @for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++)
        {
            var fill = Quarters(i);
            var x = i * 26;
            if (fill >= 1)
            {
                <path d="@Mark" transform="translate(@x,0)" class="stars__on" />
            }
            else if (fill <= 0)
            {
                <path d="@Mark" transform="translate(@x,0)" class="stars__off" />
            }
            else
            {
                @* Clip rather than gradient: a hard edge at the quarter, which is what the
                   rating actually is. A gradient stop at the same offset renders soft on
                   some engines and looked like an antialiasing fault. *@
                <clipPath id="@($"star{i}-{Key}")">
                    <rect x="0" y="0" width="@((fill * 20).ToString("0.##", Inv))" height="20" />
                </clipPath>
                <path d="@Mark" transform="translate(@x,0)" class="stars__off" />
                <g transform="translate(@x,0)" clip-path="url(#@($"star{i}-{Key}"))">
                    <path d="@Mark" class="stars__on" />
                </g>
            }
        }
    </svg>
    @if (ShowNumber)
    {
        <span class="stars__num">@Rating.ToString("F2") / 5.00</span>
        @* The word, so the band is not carried by colour alone. *@
        <span class="stars__verdict @Grades.OfStars(Rating).Tone()">@Grades.OfStars(Rating).Label()</span>
    }
</span>

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public double Rating { get; set; }
    [Parameter] public bool ShowNumber { get; set; } = true;

    private static readonly System.Globalization.CultureInfo Inv =
        System.Globalization.CultureInfo.InvariantCulture;

    private const string Mark = "M10 1.4l2.6 5.7 6.2.8-4.6 4.3 1.2 6.1-5.4-3-5.4 3 1.2-6.1L1.2 7.9l6.2-.8z";

    private const int Width = 4 * 26 + 20;

    /// <summary>
    /// Unique per instance, because two ratings on one screen would otherwise both reference
    /// the first one's clip path — SVG ids are document-global. The show report renders one of
    /// these per match.
    /// </summary>
    private readonly string Key = Guid.NewGuid().ToString("N")[..6];

    /// <summary>
    /// How much of star <paramref name="index"/> is lit, quantised to quarters so the drawing
    /// and <see cref="MatchEngineResult.GlyphsFor"/> can never disagree.
    /// </summary>
    private double Quarters(int index)
    {
        double rem = Math.Clamp(Rating - index, 0, 1);
        return rem switch
        {
            >= 0.875 => 1.0,
            >= 0.625 => 0.75,
            >= 0.375 => 0.5,
            >= 0.125 => 0.25,
            _        => 0.0
        };
    }
}
